

NC(1)               UNIX Programmer's Manual                NC(1)



NAME
     nc - arbitrary TCP connections (netcat)

SYNOPSIS
     nc [-g] host port

DESCRIPTION
     Nc opens a TCP connection to host on port and copies data
     between the connection and the standard streams.  The host may
     be a dotted-quad address or a name resolved through /etc/hosts
     and DNS (see gethostbyname(3)); port is a decimal number.

     In the default mode nc copies standard input to the socket and
     the socket to standard output, so a redirect drives it:

          nc host port < file

     An interactive console never reaches end-of-file, so use a
     redirect (or -g) rather than typing at nc directly.

     The options are:

     -g   Send the request line `GET / HTTP/1.0' followed by a blank
          line, then print the response and exit -- a one-shot HTTP
          probe that needs no standard input.

EXAMPLES
     nc bsd 23                 connect to the telnet port on `bsd'
     nc -g www.example.com 80  fetch the root document over HTTP
     nc 192.168.2.1 79 < q     send file q to the finger port

SEE ALSO
     telnet(1), host(1)

BUGS
     Only TCP (stream) connections are supported; there is no UDP
     (-u) or listen (-l) mode.



